WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:14:47.643 --> 00:14:54.229
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Please be seated.

00:15:04.415 --> 00:15:09.419
Graduates, Mary Bara,
honored guests, Faculty, staff,

00:15:09.419 --> 00:15:14.232
family, and friends,
welcome to the 2016 graduation

00:15:14.232 --> 00:15:19.732
ceremony at Stanford University's
Graduate School of Business.

00:15:19.732 --> 00:15:24.351
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:15:29.164 --> 00:15:32.190
&gt;&gt; What does your graduation signify?

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:35.606
As you know, studying at the GSB means so

00:15:35.606 --> 00:15:40.230
much more than mastering
the concepts of management,

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:44.164
it is as much about
personal transformation.

00:15:44.164 --> 00:15:48.788
As you sit before us eager
to receive your diplomas,

00:15:48.788 --> 00:15:53.517
I encourage you to think about
how you have changed And

00:15:53.517 --> 00:15:56.368
grown, during your time here.

00:15:56.368 --> 00:16:01.833
Your graduation today,
celebrates all that you have done and

00:16:01.833 --> 00:16:06.247
become, and
your readiness to have a dramatic and

00:16:06.247 --> 00:16:11.817
positive impact on the organizations
you will lead, manage or

00:16:11.817 --> 00:16:18.150
found and through those organizations,
the communities they serve.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:25.430
What you have accomplished at the GSP,
you have not accomplished alone.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:30.440
You've learned and received help,
guidance, mentorship and

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:35.780
support from many quarters,
all of which are represented here, today.

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:40.350
First, you're sitting shoulder

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:44.860
to shoulder with your classmates,
as you have throughout your time here.

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:50.740
Think how much you've learned from
one another in squads, clubs,

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:54.500
study trips small group dinners, talk,

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:59.870
touchy feely, or
just in quiet conversation together.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:05.950
You forged bonds here that will
become lifelong friendships,

00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:09.060
which will nurture and
sustain you in the years ahead.

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:13.096
Take a moment to show your
appreciation for one another.

00:17:13.096 --> 00:17:18.061
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Sitting behind me on the stage

00:17:18.061 --> 00:17:22.594
is a group of faculty, who represent

00:17:22.594 --> 00:17:28.007
the more than 200 tenure line faculty and

00:17:28.007 --> 00:17:33.858
practitioners, who have
been your teachers,

00:17:33.858 --> 00:17:39.561
coaches, mentors, study trip companions,

00:17:39.561 --> 00:17:44.120
career advisers, and so much more.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:46.760
I would ask the faculty to stand so

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:51.225
that your students can
express their appreciation.

00:17:51.225 --> 00:17:55.623
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:17:55.623 --> 00:18:00.044
&gt;&gt; And at various locations

00:18:00.044 --> 00:18:05.729
throughout the amphitheater,

00:18:05.729 --> 00:18:11.200
our staff from the MBA, MSX and

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:16.464
PhD programs, without their

00:18:16.464 --> 00:18:23.622
tireless efforts on behalf of all of us,

00:18:23.622 --> 00:18:30.590
none of us would be celebrating today.

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:33.980
I ask the staff to stand,
if they are not already standing.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:35.636
Wherever they are and be recognized.

00:18:35.636 --> 00:18:43.701
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I have left the most important for

00:18:43.701 --> 00:18:48.556
last, each of you has been

00:18:48.556 --> 00:18:54.956
supported by family and friends,

00:18:54.956 --> 00:19:02.030
as you will be throughout your lives.

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:08.470
They provided encouragement,
validation, love, and

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:11.310
yes, in many cases,
financial support as well.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:17.810
While we will hand the diplomas to you,
you know that in parenthesis

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:22.020
after your name, belong the names
of those who have nurtured you and

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:23.030
supported you along the way.

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:27.979
This is your opportunity to stand and
say thank you to your friends and

00:19:27.979 --> 00:19:32.859
family, who are here to celebrate
your accomplishments with you.

00:19:32.859 --> 00:19:42.859
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:19:54.953 --> 00:20:00.505
&gt;&gt; Our mission is

00:20:00.505 --> 00:20:07.155
different from that most business schools.

00:20:07.155 --> 00:20:09.215
Our focus is on transformation,

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:15.047
we are privileged to be the most
selective business school in the world.

00:20:15.047 --> 00:20:17.280
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:22.230
&gt;&gt; In making our selections, we try to
pick the highest potential young men and

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:26.220
women, and
to provide them with an education

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:31.090
that will enable them to go on to
live lives of impact and meaning.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:36.340
I hope that this has been
a transformative experience for you and

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:40.540
that you have become infected
by the GSB's lofty ambitions for

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:45.530
you and that you will indeed
take this education, and

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:48.350
your desire to have
an impact out with you.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:55.870
I recently returned from East Africa,
where I'd gone for the opening for

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:57.595
our new seed center in Nairobi.

00:20:57.595 --> 00:21:04.450
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I love being in Africa,

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:09.280
but one of the things it so
clearly reminds me of

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:13.760
is how many people in the world
live in very dire circumstances.

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:21.930
And let's not kid ourselves that the
difference between where we sit today and

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.920
people with exactly the same talents

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:29.160
in the slums of Kibera is
entirely of our own making.

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:36.240
Each of us has helped along the way and
has been a recipient of good fortune,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:41.120
whether by birth or
the assistance of others, with that comes

00:21:41.120 --> 00:21:45.650
a responsibility to make the most of
the opportunities that we have been given.

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:52.490
It has become my custom to send you
off with three wishes that I have for

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:53.130
you in your future.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:59.070
Here are my hopes for the class of 2016.

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:03.780
First, do not be confused
about what really matters.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:07.320
It is the most important relationships
in your life, that give it meaning.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:10.400
Make sure that you nurture them.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:13.530
They will sustain you in
the good times and the bad.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:18.770
Second, be a lifelong learner.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:21.900
Life is a marathon, not a sprint.

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:28.380
Life expectancy is going up, and you will
likely want to work for a very long time.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:34.479
So settle in for a long journey and
constantly invest in your self

00:22:34.479 --> 00:22:39.330
knowledge and capabilities,
both professional and personal, so

00:22:39.330 --> 00:22:43.360
that you're increasingly well equipped for
the years that still lie ahead.

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:53.310
Finally, let me note that at a recent 30th
GSB reunion, 82% of the class showed up.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:58.478
I want you to reflect on that for
a moment, 82%.

00:22:58.478 --> 00:23:01.570
So look around, make this

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:06.820
group of people an important part of
your life for the rest of your life.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:10.004
Stay connected to one another and to us.

00:23:13.903 --> 00:23:17.627
Our graduation speaker today is an alumni,

00:23:17.627 --> 00:23:22.862
who embodies these values while
leading one of the largest and

00:23:22.862 --> 00:23:27.820
most important companies in the world,
General Motors.

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:28.690
In 2015,

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:34.130
she was ranked first on Fortune's list of
the 50 most powerful women in business.

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:37.394
And just this week,
she was named as number five on

00:23:37.394 --> 00:23:41.070
the Forbes list of the most
powerful women in the world.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:42.960
As well as number one on their list

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:45.872
on their list of the most
powerful women in business

00:23:45.872 --> 00:23:56.177
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:23:56.177 --> 00:23:58.898
&gt;&gt; Mary Barra graduated from

00:23:58.898 --> 00:24:03.232
the Stanford GSB with an MBA in 1990.

00:24:03.232 --> 00:24:10.242
She was an Arjay Miller Scholar and
I'm so pleased that at age 100.

00:24:10.242 --> 00:24:11.084
Yes, folks.

00:24:11.084 --> 00:24:16.039
That's 100, that Arjay is with
us today to recognize this

00:24:16.039 --> 00:24:18.723
year's Arjay Miller Scholars.

00:24:18.723 --> 00:24:20.823
Please give a round of applause to Arjay.

00:24:20.823 --> 00:24:30.823
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:24:33.938 --> 00:24:37.193
&gt;&gt; Apart from her two years at the GSB,

00:24:37.193 --> 00:24:41.544
Mary has pretty much been all GM,
all the time.

00:24:41.544 --> 00:24:46.349
She started as a General Motors Institute
co-op student at

00:24:46.349 --> 00:24:53.087
the Pontiac Motor Division, earning
a BS in electrical engineering in 1985.

00:24:53.087 --> 00:24:55.710
After a variety of other roles,

00:24:55.710 --> 00:25:01.161
Mary was put in charge of global
product development In 2011.

00:25:01.161 --> 00:25:08.652
And so, we see the fruits of her vision
and leadership on the road everyday.

00:25:08.652 --> 00:25:13.314
We have gotten to know Mary and her
leadership very well in the last few years

00:25:13.314 --> 00:25:17.833
as we have worked together on
a transformational leadership program for

00:25:17.833 --> 00:25:21.703
senior executives, and
high potentials at General Motors.

00:25:21.703 --> 00:25:26.048
We take about 35 of these
incredible executives and

00:25:26.048 --> 00:25:31.746
put them together with GSB faculty
5 times a year at various locations

00:25:31.746 --> 00:25:37.060
around the world, culminating
in a group-based action project

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:42.201
that addresses present strategic
issues facing the company.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:49.330
In this context, I've gotten to observe
Mary's leadership style in person.

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:53.437
She leads like a GSPer.

00:25:53.437 --> 00:26:00.973
She's self-aware, inclusive,
open, direct and decisive.

00:26:00.973 --> 00:26:05.266
In short, she's an iconic
exemplar of the goals we had for

00:26:05.266 --> 00:26:07.638
each of you in leadership labs.

00:26:11.054 --> 00:26:14.688
Despite the rigors of her role, Mary and
her husband, Tony make time for

00:26:14.688 --> 00:26:15.650
their community.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:23.010
As examples in 2014, they chaired
the Detroit International Wine Auction,

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:28.370
raising a record $2.4 million for
scholarships and community arts programs.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:35.779
And in 2012, they chaired
the Barbara Ann Karmanos Center

00:26:35.779 --> 00:26:41.628
30th Annual Dinner,
raising nearly $1.7 million for

00:26:41.628 --> 00:26:46.924
cancer research and
Mary Feinstein for Stanford too.

00:26:46.924 --> 00:26:52.981
I'm so proud to have her as a member of my
advisory council while at the same time,

00:26:52.981 --> 00:26:58.594
she serves on what we at the GSP like
to think of as Stanford's other board,

00:26:58.594 --> 00:27:02.002
the Stanford University Board of Trustees.

00:27:03.825 --> 00:27:06.747
It is a pleasure to have
Mary with us today.

00:27:06.747 --> 00:27:10.433
Please join me in giving a warm
GSB welcome to Mary Barra.

00:27:10.433 --> 00:27:20.338
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:27:25.406 --> 00:27:26.507
&gt;&gt; Dean Saloner,

00:27:26.507 --> 00:27:31.927
Dean Emeritus Arajay Miller, members
of the Stanford Board of Trustees and

00:27:31.927 --> 00:27:37.187
the GSB advisory council,
distinguished faculty and administration.

00:27:37.187 --> 00:27:41.629
Honored guests, parents, family, friends.

00:27:41.629 --> 00:27:46.305
And most importantly,
members of the class of 2016.

00:27:46.305 --> 00:27:49.407
Thank you for
having me here this afternoon.

00:27:49.407 --> 00:27:54.176
I am truly honored to be a part
of your commencement ceremony.

00:27:54.176 --> 00:27:59.033
Dean Saloner as an alumna and a member of
the GSB Advisory Council, I want to thank

00:27:59.033 --> 00:28:03.835
you for your significant contributions
to this school and to General Motors.

00:28:03.835 --> 00:28:11.991
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:28:11.991 --> 00:28:16.850
to the graduates, let me say that
it seems like a lifetime ago.

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:21.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, that's not going to work.

00:28:21.350 --> 00:28:25.601
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It does seem

00:28:25.601 --> 00:28:29.365
like a lifetime ago when I
sat where you sit today.

00:28:29.365 --> 00:28:33.849
I recall very well, the promise and
the excitement of commencement and

00:28:33.849 --> 00:28:35.726
I am thrilled for each of you.

00:28:35.726 --> 00:28:37.183
So, congratulations.

00:28:37.183 --> 00:28:45.317
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Everything we

00:28:45.317 --> 00:28:50.949
know about the class of 2016 tells us that
you are one of the most accomplished and

00:28:50.949 --> 00:28:53.897
diverse classes in the school's history.

00:28:53.897 --> 00:28:56.308
42% of your class are women.

00:28:56.308 --> 00:29:05.092
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:29:05.092 --> 00:29:08.960
23% are US minorities,
both all time highs.

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:16.054
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; International

00:29:16.054 --> 00:29:19.236
students represent 44%, also a new high.

00:29:19.236 --> 00:29:25.778
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; In fact,

00:29:25.778 --> 00:29:30.625
members of your class represent
62 different countries and

00:29:30.625 --> 00:29:36.494
I got to spend some time with a few of
you yesterday at a breakfast meeting and

00:29:36.494 --> 00:29:42.297
I have to tell you, what I most
appreciated was the diversity of thought.

00:29:42.297 --> 00:29:46.823
As a class, you have previous experience
in more than 300 organizations.

00:29:46.823 --> 00:29:51.466
Everything from consulting and
private equity to manufacturing and

00:29:51.466 --> 00:29:56.520
the military, to government,
education and nonprofit organizations.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:01.219
There is no doubt that
you are well-prepared and

00:30:01.219 --> 00:30:05.890
well-positioned to fulfill the GSP's
vision to change the world.

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:11.723
Of course, the world has changed
considerably since I graduated.

00:30:11.723 --> 00:30:18.693
When I graduated in 1990, the Dow Jones
Industrial Average closed above 29,000 for

00:30:18.693 --> 00:30:24.354
the first time and we were all amazed
with something that was being introduced

00:30:24.354 --> 00:30:30.213
called the World Wide Web and there was no
such thing as a smart phone or texting.

00:30:30.213 --> 00:30:35.641
If you were an early adapter in 1990,
you had what was called a flip phone and

00:30:35.641 --> 00:30:38.720
you used it to actually make phone calls,
but

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:43.070
not everything has changed
since I was a student.

00:30:43.070 --> 00:30:47.810
Just as it is today, Stanford was
still the finest school in the world

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:49.940
with a faculty that was second to none.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:52.169
I know this,

00:30:52.169 --> 00:30:56.500
because many of you have studied with some
of the same professors who taught me.

00:30:56.500 --> 00:31:01.490
I'm very happy today to see
Professor George Foster who

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:04.363
was my teacher for accounting.

00:31:04.363 --> 00:31:09.004
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I'm also thrilled to

00:31:09.004 --> 00:31:13.488
see Professor James VanHorne who came
out of retirement last spring to teach

00:31:13.488 --> 00:31:14.552
Finance 211.

00:31:14.552 --> 00:31:20.659
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And what I find most remarkable is that

00:31:20.659 --> 00:31:26.170
an updated textbook by the late Professor
Charles Hornbrook is still used today.

00:31:27.210 --> 00:31:31.010
My point is that you and
I learned from the best, the people

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:36.510
who literally wrote the books and
now the e-books on business management.

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:40.979
And so, it is no surprise to me that
you are extremely well-prepared for

00:31:40.979 --> 00:31:43.483
the road that stretches out before you.

00:31:43.483 --> 00:31:47.308
But as one who has traveled
some distance on that road,

00:31:47.308 --> 00:31:51.882
I can also tell you what you learn
here is just the beginning of what

00:31:51.882 --> 00:31:55.310
you'll need to know for
success going forward.

00:31:55.310 --> 00:32:00.070
When I came to Stanford, I quickly
learned I didn't know what I didn't know.

00:32:00.070 --> 00:32:01.516
I was 26 years old.

00:32:01.516 --> 00:32:04.947
I'd lived my entire life
in Southeast Michigan and

00:32:04.947 --> 00:32:07.993
I worked for
General Motors since I was 18.

00:32:07.993 --> 00:32:11.606
&gt;From accounting to corporate
culture to entrepreneurship,

00:32:11.606 --> 00:32:17.260
my eyes were opened and I learned to see
the world from a different perspective.

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:22.920
My experiences on this campus changed
my life and accelerated my career.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:26.920
They prepared me to manage and
ultimately to lead.

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:32.923
So allow me to share four leadership
lessons that I have learned since

00:32:32.923 --> 00:32:33.933
graduation.

00:32:33.933 --> 00:32:39.192
Lessons that have been important to me and
I hope will be of some value to you.

00:32:39.192 --> 00:32:42.426
First, leaders listen.

00:32:42.426 --> 00:32:46.179
You are graduating from one of the finest
business schools in the world.

00:32:46.179 --> 00:32:49.057
Be proud of that, but also be humble and

00:32:49.057 --> 00:32:54.039
know that any significant
accomplishment is always a team effort.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.780
It's okay to admit when you don't know.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:00.280
It's okay to ask for help and

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:03.920
it's more than okay to listen
to the people you lead.

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:05.490
In fact, it's essential.

00:33:06.530 --> 00:33:11.171
Some years ago, I was asked to lead GM's
Global Human Resources Function during

00:33:11.171 --> 00:33:13.092
a critical time for the company.

00:33:13.092 --> 00:33:17.571
I had never worked in HR before, but
I was confident I could make a difference.

00:33:17.571 --> 00:33:22.058
I accepted the position and
I immediately began to identify areas I

00:33:22.058 --> 00:33:27.680
believed that we could improve, and
help the company’s performance.

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:32.230
Pretty quickly, I came to the conclusion
that the company’s vacation policy was too

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:32.873
generous.

00:33:32.873 --> 00:33:37.472
I was troubled by a program that allowed
employees to take additional vacation days

00:33:37.472 --> 00:33:41.161
beyond what they had earned by
purchasing these additional days.

00:33:41.161 --> 00:33:44.690
So, I propose to my staff
to eliminate the program.

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:48.991
They told me it was a bad idea.

00:33:48.991 --> 00:33:51.813
I argued that the program
had out lead to usefulness.

00:33:51.813 --> 00:33:56.512
No longer a place and are more leaner
more nimble, more competitive company.

00:33:56.512 --> 00:34:00.413
And so, I eliminated the program.

00:34:00.413 --> 00:34:03.927
The next day,
the employees nearly eliminated me.

00:34:03.927 --> 00:34:07.906
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What I did not fully understand,

00:34:07.906 --> 00:34:12.083
because I did not listen to my
team was that employees purchase

00:34:12.083 --> 00:34:16.117
these days to give them flexibility
in their work and life.

00:34:16.117 --> 00:34:20.423
What's more, employees often use
these times at fractions at a time,

00:34:20.423 --> 00:34:22.091
a few hours here and there.

00:34:22.091 --> 00:34:25.957
So they could attend to personal
matters like caring for

00:34:25.957 --> 00:34:30.151
an elderly parent or
attending a child's sporting even, or

00:34:30.151 --> 00:34:33.536
attending a spouse's
commencement ceremony.

00:34:33.536 --> 00:34:36.650
In the world of laptops and
smartphones and

00:34:36.650 --> 00:34:42.285
constant connectivity where employees
can and do work anytime, anywhere.

00:34:42.285 --> 00:34:47.155
The flexibility to purchase those
additional vacation days was viewed by

00:34:47.155 --> 00:34:50.620
many employees as an extremely
important benefit.

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:56.890
Long story short,
Irene stated the policy very quickly and

00:34:56.890 --> 00:35:00.420
I learned I needed to listen much
more carefully in the future.

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:06.770
I also learned, it's very important to
surround yourself with people who will

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:10.677
tell you when and why you're wrong and
I learned that listening

00:35:10.677 --> 00:35:14.607
doesn't diminish when you become
the general manager or CEO.

00:35:14.607 --> 00:35:17.482
Frankly, it increases.

00:35:17.482 --> 00:35:21.245
Lesson two, leaders care.

00:35:21.245 --> 00:35:25.663
I was a student here in the late
1980s where MBAs were frequently

00:35:25.663 --> 00:35:27.446
compared to Gordon Gekko,

00:35:27.446 --> 00:35:33.140
a character played by Michael Douglas
in the 1987 film Wall Street.

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:36.420
Gordon Gekko's mantra was simple,
greed is good.

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:41.382
Today, the comparisons are more likely
to be with characters in the Wolf of

00:35:41.382 --> 00:35:43.000
Wall Street or the Big Short.

00:35:44.060 --> 00:35:48.710
But unfortunately, despite the fact that
business leaders have come a long way

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:50.710
since the stereotype of Gordon Gekko,

00:35:51.740 --> 00:35:56.490
many people continue to view business
with a considerable disdain.

00:35:57.700 --> 00:36:02.540
Gallup's latest update on confidence
in US Institutions from July of 15

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:08.790
reports that more than three-quarters
of US adults don't trust big business.

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:14.037
In fact, the only institution that ranked
lower than big business was Congress,

00:36:14.037 --> 00:36:14.883
but come on.

00:36:14.883 --> 00:36:15.787
That's like saying,

00:36:15.787 --> 00:36:18.318
you're the least ambitious
character in the Game of Thrones.

00:36:18.318 --> 00:36:23.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As leaders in business, government,

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:27.224
nonprofits, NGOs or
wherever your career takes you,

00:36:27.224 --> 00:36:31.772
you have a responsibility to
help change the relationship and

00:36:31.772 --> 00:36:35.453
the reputation institutions
have with society.

00:36:35.453 --> 00:36:37.116
Where do we start?

00:36:37.116 --> 00:36:41.144
For me at General Motors,
it starts with the customer.

00:36:41.144 --> 00:36:45.244
Now about two and a half years ago,
it was a Friday afternoon.

00:36:45.244 --> 00:36:49.723
I was just about ready to close my
laptop and slide it into my bag, and

00:36:49.723 --> 00:36:51.580
an email came in.

00:36:51.580 --> 00:36:52.640
It was from a customer.

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:56.010
He was very upset.

00:36:56.010 --> 00:37:00.620
He wanted to get a loaner that the company
had promised and he was unable to get it.

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:02.934
He was upset with his vehicle.

00:37:02.934 --> 00:37:06.278
He was upset with the company and
he was pretty darn upset with me, and

00:37:06.278 --> 00:37:07.150
he let me know it.

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:12.090
So I stared at my computer for
a minute and then I responded and said,

00:37:12.090 --> 00:37:14.030
would you please share your phone number?

00:37:15.030 --> 00:37:17.604
A minute or two later,
I had his phone number.

00:37:17.604 --> 00:37:20.943
So, I picked up the phone and
I gave him a call.

00:37:20.943 --> 00:37:22.917
He explained to me what had happened.

00:37:22.917 --> 00:37:27.506
How the dealership had let him down and
how we weren't fulfilling his promise.

00:37:27.506 --> 00:37:30.463
I promised him that I would
make sure he got a loaner and

00:37:30.463 --> 00:37:32.714
I immediately called the right people.

00:37:32.714 --> 00:37:36.708
And by the next morning,
he had the loaner vehicle we had promised.

00:37:36.708 --> 00:37:37.420
I felt good.

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:38.910
I thought problem resolved.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:42.752
But on Monday morning,
I got another email.

00:37:42.752 --> 00:37:47.873
It was from the same customer and he said,
I couldn't believe you responded.

00:37:47.873 --> 00:37:50.066
I couldn't believe you cared.

00:37:50.066 --> 00:37:53.943
I couldn't believe how well
the dealership and your team treated me.

00:37:53.943 --> 00:37:57.870
I have my loaner and I will now
be a General Motors customer for

00:37:57.870 --> 00:37:59.259
the reset of my life.

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:05.730
The takeaway for me is that you and
your company will make mistakes.

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:11.575
How you deal with those mistakes will
largely determine how you'll be judged.

00:38:11.575 --> 00:38:14.687
And no matter what business you're in,

00:38:14.687 --> 00:38:18.633
you only win when the customer tells you,
you win.

00:38:18.633 --> 00:38:21.361
Lesson three, leaders inspire.

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:27.570
As I said, your company’s success depends
on how well you satisfy customers, but

00:38:27.570 --> 00:38:31.490
changing the world goes well
beyond serving customers.

00:38:31.490 --> 00:38:34.743
It’s also about serving society.

00:38:34.743 --> 00:38:38.255
When one day soon,
you will be leading an organization.

00:38:38.255 --> 00:38:42.059
You should absolutely be
concerned about the bottom line.

00:38:42.059 --> 00:38:46.732
In today's world, you will certainly
be measured by quarterly results.

00:38:46.732 --> 00:38:52.055
But as a visionary leader, you should be
thinking more than just the next quarter.

00:38:52.055 --> 00:38:57.033
You should be thinking about the next
decade and what were your companies

00:38:57.033 --> 00:39:01.448
reputation and place in the world
be forty-quarters from now?

00:39:01.448 --> 00:39:06.046
More and more, today's employees want
to be connected to a broader purpose,

00:39:06.046 --> 00:39:07.168
a higher calling.

00:39:07.168 --> 00:39:09.108
They want their companies and

00:39:09.108 --> 00:39:12.606
their institutions to make
the world a better place.

00:39:12.606 --> 00:39:15.491
I believe we can do both.

00:39:15.491 --> 00:39:18.531
I believe we are required to do both and

00:39:18.531 --> 00:39:24.421
I believe it's up to leaders like you
to set the tone, create the vision,

00:39:24.421 --> 00:39:30.220
inspire the behaviors that allow
organizations to best serve society.

00:39:31.450 --> 00:39:35.468
As leaders, we have an obligation
to act responsibly and

00:39:35.468 --> 00:39:40.322
courageously for the people and for
the planet, because our actions

00:39:40.322 --> 00:39:45.030
do have long-term effects on
the generations that come after us.

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:49.188
It means creating workplaces where
everyone is empowered and valued, and

00:39:49.188 --> 00:39:52.640
can contribute to his or
her full potential.

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:56.207
It means working to improve
the communities where we live and work.

00:39:56.207 --> 00:39:59.490
For example by supporting and
improving STEM education.

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:05.040
It means changing the way we build
our products, using renewable energy,

00:40:05.040 --> 00:40:10.560
creating landfill-free facilities,
and working to address climate change.

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:13.500
And at GMs, it means working to reduce and

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:17.470
one day eliminate the effect our
cars have on the environment.

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:23.060
At the end of the day,
all businesses are about people first,

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:27.670
because the only way we can genuinely
successfully build businesses,

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:33.040
is by building lasting relationships
both inside and outside of the company.

00:40:33.040 --> 00:40:36.600
And at GM we do that by
holding our self accountable,

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:39.150
doing what we say we're going to do, and

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:43.610
working hard to inspire others to strive
for something bigger than themselves.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:48.000
And my final lesson, leaders work.

00:40:49.120 --> 00:40:53.510
Your Stanford degree tells others
you have talent and that’s great.

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:59.330
But if you truly want to change
the world you need more than talent.

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:01.970
You also need to do the work.

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:06.110
Because hard work beats talent
if talent doesn't work hard.

00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:09.580
I think about my mom and dad.

00:41:09.580 --> 00:41:12.600
My parents grew up in
the Great Depression.

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:16.380
My mom grew up on a farm
in Northern Michigan.

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:20.790
My dad grew up in an iron
mining town in upper Minnesota.

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:23.430
They didn't have many advantages.

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:28.690
They each only had high school degrees,
but they believed in the American dream.

00:41:29.750 --> 00:41:32.760
And they worked hard to achieve it.

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:37.070
They taught my brother and me that there
is no substitute for hard work and

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:38.390
you work before you play.

00:41:39.520 --> 00:41:44.120
And like so many other parents and
grandparents here today, they displayed

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:49.210
the kind of passion and grit that allowed
them to not just raise a family, but

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:52.750
to build a foundation from which
we could reach even higher.

00:41:54.130 --> 00:41:57.400
Your education will open doors.

00:41:57.400 --> 00:42:00.828
Your talents will open worlds.

00:42:00.828 --> 00:42:04.360
But your hard work will
enable you to accomplish more

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:06.010
than you ever thought possible.

00:42:07.720 --> 00:42:11.254
So a lot has changed since
the years I sat in your chair, but

00:42:11.254 --> 00:42:14.720
the responsibilities of
leadership have stayed the same.

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:19.240
Each of you are remarkably capable.

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:22.080
You are highly trained and well schooled.

00:42:22.080 --> 00:42:27.100
And you are alive at an extraordinary
time in human history, where technology

00:42:27.100 --> 00:42:31.750
is changing our lives faster and
more profoundly than ever before.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:36.190
In this world of unbounded opportunity,

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:40.070
all of you have the opportunity
to be successful.

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:44.330
So my question to you is how
will you define success?

00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:47.550
Will you challenge the status quo?

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:52.120
Will you inspire others
to do great things?

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:55.790
Will you protect what needs protecting?

00:42:56.910 --> 00:43:02.040
And will you build a foundation that
allows future generations to reach

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:02.590
even higher?

00:43:03.690 --> 00:43:07.440
In short, will you leave the world
better than you found it?

00:43:09.110 --> 00:43:12.700
I, along with so many others,
are counting on you.

00:43:14.270 --> 00:43:15.550
Again thank you so much.

00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:20.663
It's been wonderful to be here and
share this celebration,

00:43:20.663 --> 00:43:24.691
and congratulations to the class of 2016.

00:43:24.691 --> 00:43:34.691
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:43:37.564 --> 00:43:39.032
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Mary.

00:43:39.032 --> 00:43:46.240
It is now my great honor to
introduce the GSP PhD class of 2016.

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:52.220
The research undertaken by these
students is truly impressive,

00:43:52.220 --> 00:43:55.330
both in its quality and its importance.

00:43:55.330 --> 00:44:01.590
In aggregate, this research represents
over 100 person years of intense work.

00:44:01.590 --> 00:44:03.910
Let me briefly give some examples,

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:08.010
showing the broad scope of issues
these students have addressed.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.480
The research of several of these
students involves studying networks.

00:44:11.480 --> 00:44:16.120
Among other things, this work explored
how politicians are held accountable

00:44:16.120 --> 00:44:21.070
by the structure of social networks, how
the strategic networks of venture capital

00:44:21.070 --> 00:44:25.450
firms affect the performance of
the companies that they fund.

00:44:25.450 --> 00:44:31.005
How the social network connections of
entrepreneurs affect the flow of ideas and

00:44:31.005 --> 00:44:33.300
the formation of teams.

00:44:33.300 --> 00:44:38.370
And how in general, economic decisions
are affected by social networks.

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:44.660
Other students address the role that
privilege plays in maintaining inequality,

00:44:44.660 --> 00:44:50.170
and examine how conflicts in organizations
affect organizational learning.

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:53.090
One student explored how
employment contracts

00:44:53.090 --> 00:44:57.760
can be designed to make sure the projects
aren't inefficiently prolonged.

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:02.390
Another studied the effects of
competition on supply chains, and

00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:05.030
another looked at how
the availability of collateral

00:45:05.030 --> 00:45:08.580
determines the funding
strategies of small firms.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:12.625
Several students in accounting looked at
how incentives and reputational concerns

00:45:12.625 --> 00:45:17.340
affect what gets disclosed by those
firms and when it gets disclosed.

00:45:17.340 --> 00:45:21.660
One accounting student examined how
the desires of security analysts to mimic

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:25.360
their peers affect the quality
of their forecast.

00:45:25.360 --> 00:45:29.510
Finally, in the marketing area one student
studied how people respond to conflicting

00:45:29.510 --> 00:45:34.680
information given in product reviews
like those found on Amazon and Yelp.

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:38.960
Another studied how the advertising of
e-cigarettes affects the demand for

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:40.650
traditional cigarettes.

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:44.150
And another explored how
the nearly constant interruptions

00:45:44.150 --> 00:45:48.740
consumers encounter in their daily lives,
affects decision making and

00:45:48.740 --> 00:45:51.710
attempts at consumer persuasion.

00:45:51.710 --> 00:45:55.380
All of this work is rigorously
done in accord with the highest

00:45:55.380 --> 00:45:57.220
academic standards, and

00:45:57.220 --> 00:46:02.680
all of it adds in important ways to our
understanding of management and policy.

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:07.320
Today, we celebrate the transition
of these PhD candidates,

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:11.040
from students to future
intellectual leaders.

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:16.573
After their advisors place the hood of
the Stanford Business School on them, they

00:46:16.573 --> 00:46:21.881
will join us on the stage as we welcome
them into our profession as colleagues.

